<section class="rd-sec rd-hero"><div class="wrap"><div class="hero-split">
<div><p class="kicker">Temperature</p>
<h1>Heat sits in the<br>top five centimetres.</h1>
<p class="lede">A pocket spring core is mostly air. What holds warmth is the layer directly under you, and on ours that layer comes out.</p>
<div class="cta-row"><a class="btn" href="#configure">Choose your size</a><a class="btn btn--ghost btn-line" href="{{ pages['specification'].url | default: '/pages/specification' }}">See the build</a></div></div>
<figure class="figure ">{% render 'rd-img', file: 'coolknit-macro.webp', alt: "Cool-knit cover, macro", width: 1400, height: 781, loading: 'eager', fetchpriority: 'high' %}</figure>
</div></div></section>

<section class="rd-sec rd-sec--bone"><div class="wrap"><div class="narrow">
<div class="sec-head"><p class="kicker">Three answers</p><h2>Cooling built into the structure, not sprayed onto it.</h2></div>
<p>Foam holds heat because it is solid and it wraps. Our comfort stack is built to move air through itself, and the core beneath is largely open.</p>
<div class="pull"><p>If it sleeps warm, you change five centimetres. Not the bed.</p></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="rd-sec"><div class="wrap">
<div class="sec-head narrow" style="text-align:center"><p class="kicker">How it stays cool</p><h2>Three layers doing it</h2></div>
<div class="grid-3" style="margin-bottom:0">
<div><p class="mono" style="color:var(--ember);margin-bottom:10px">400 gram</p><h3>Cool-knit cover</h3><p>Knitted rather than laminated. Air passes through the surface instead of being held against it by a bonded film.</p></div>
<div><p class="mono" style="color:var(--ember);margin-bottom:10px">1cm &middot; 28 density</p><h3>Gel-infused memory foam</h3><p>Gel is what lets memory foam contour to a shoulder without holding the warmth that contouring normally creates.</p></div>
<div><p class="mono" style="color:var(--ember);margin-bottom:10px">2.5cm &middot; wave cut</p><h3>Wave-profile foam</h3><p>Cut into a wave, so channels run through the middle of the comfort stack. Warmth moves sideways and leaves rather than gathering between two solid layers.</p></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="rd-sec rd-sec--bone"><div class="wrap"><div class="split">
<figure class="figure ">{% render 'rd-img', file: 'quilt-edge.webp', alt: "Wave-profile cut visible at the edge", width: 1400, height: 781, loading: 'lazy' %}</figure>
<div><p class="kicker">And beneath it</p><h2>Twenty centimetres<br>of mostly air.</h2>
<p style="margin-top:20px">A seven-zone pocket spring core is an open structure. Air moves between every coil, so warmth leaves downward as well as upward instead of being trapped by a solid slab of foam.</p>
<p>It is the reason a sprung mattress sleeps cooler than an all-foam one before anything is added to it.</p></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="rd-sec"><div class="wrap"><div class="narrow">
<p class="kicker">Adjust to Desire</p><h2>Still sleeping warm?<br>We remake the layer.</h2>
<p style="margin-top:22px">Sleep on it for a month. If it still runs warmer than you want, tell us and we build a new comfort layer to that, with our compliments. Worth {{ layer_price | default: '£299' }}. Nothing goes back and nothing is disassembled.</p>
</div></div></section>

